{"id": "e76dbc08-f25c-4542-ab22-e678eb5cd826", "text": "Q: Failed to execute goal org.apache.maven.plugins:maven-compiler-plugin:3.1,Cannot access javax.annotation.concurrent.NotThreadSafe A: <p>Find some files from SQL programs and Discord the ESSR/- Files to the desktop. Here are some tips</p>\n<p>FIle} open</p>\n<p>Desk)- Timed Cocurent</p>\n<p>Elementary Drilon/ Execute on all orders over the Open File.</p>\n<p>CSS command prompt to be not kept open.</p>\n", "tags": ["java", "maven", "file-not-found", "maven-compiler-plugin"]}
{"id": "fbf6c0d5-3a0e-4491-8124-5e2349a33b79", "text": "Q: Explain this instruction set and the Z register A: <p>The <a href=\"http://www.atmel.com/Images/doc8008.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">document you linked</a> describes the purpose of the Z register:</p>\n<blockquote>\nSix of the 32 registers can be used as three 16-bit indirect address register > pointers for Data\nSpace addressing \u2013 enabling efficient address calculations. One of the these\naddress pointers\ncan also be used as an address pointer for look up tables in Flash program\nmemory. These\nadded function registers are the 16-bit X-, Y-, and Z-register, described\nlater in this section.\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3>The X-register, Y-register, and Z-register</h3>\nThe registers R26:R31 have some added functions to their general purpose usage.\nThese registers\nare 16-bit address pointers for indirect addressing of the data space. [...]\n<br/><br/>\nIn the different addressing modes these address registers have functions as fixed displacement,\nautomatic increment, and automatic decrement (see the instruction set reference for details).\n</blockquote>\n<p>The intent of the the code example is to load the Z register with the address of a byte at offset R20 in <code>seg_table</code>. Your example example is incomplete; following the example code would be an <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmel_AVR_instruction_set#Instruction_timing\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">LPM</a> instruction that fetched the byte from program memory.</p>\n<p>The address of <code>segtable</code> needs to be shifted over to the left (the equivalent of multiplying by two), because the assembler/linker treats the program memory as being word (16-bit) addressable, while the LPM instruction addresses program memory by bytes. In other words, to the assembler, the address of <code>segtable</code> is an offset measured in 16-bit words from the start of program memory. To the LPM instruction the value contained in the Z register is interpreted as an offset measured in 8-bit bytes from the start of program memory. To convert the address of <code>segtable</code> to an offset usable with LPM it needs to multiplied by two.</p>\n<p>If it's not clear, values in the <code>segtable</code> are bitmasks for a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven-segment_display\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">seven-segment LED display</a>.</p>\n", "tags": ["assembly", "avr"]}
{"id": "1f55f2fa-b443-4ab3-989c-7502a0dd9db4", "text": "Q: How can I generate a &quot;Social Golfer&quot; matrix for worker seating arrangement? A: <p>You say you need</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>an APL function, or MS Access VBA function, which takes as arguments the total number of employees, total number of dinning tables, and number of employees per dinning table for generating rotating seating assignments.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If your requirement is that the resulting arrangement should fulfill your requirements <em>exactly</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A person cannot be seated again with other persons they have sat with in the past until all possible permutations have been exhausted.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Then your goal is very ambitious indeed. The general problem your function would be trying to solve is <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NP-hardness\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">NP-hard</a>, so finding an algorithm that works and completes in a reasonable time for all input parameters is likely impossible.</p>\n<p>I'm therefore focusing on your concrete problem at hand, with the parameters you mentioned:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>50 tables\n280 people\nat most, 6 people per table</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Beyond that, your question leaves a little bit of room for interpretation since <code>50 * 6 = 300</code> and you're saying you only have <strong>280</strong> people to distribute. I assume you want people to sit in groups of either <strong>5</strong> or <strong>6</strong> since <strong>280</strong> is not divisible by <strong>6</strong>. Furthermore, since <strong>280</strong> people can be partitioned into <strong>45</strong> groups of <strong>6</strong> and <strong>2</strong> of <strong>5</strong>, I assumed that it is ok if some tables stay unused during some of the sessions aswell.</p>\n<p>Your next rule is the tricky one:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>RULE: Unique sets of 6 people are required for each workday. A person cannot be seated again with other persons they have sat with in the past until all possible permutations have been exhausted.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The only way you're going to get even close to a solution schedule that fulfills this requirements is by using techniques from combinatorial design theory.</p>\n<p>For your particular parameters, I did not find a schedule that fulfills this requirement, but i found one that comes <em>extremely</em> close.</p>\n<p>The schedule has the following properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Everyone meets everyone over the course of <strong>59</strong> sessions. This means, the <code>(280 * 279) / 2 = 39060</code> possible pairings are all covered.</li>\n<li>Of the <code>39060</code> pairings, only <code>970</code> (<code>~2.5%</code>) occur twice. No pairing occurs 3 times or more.</li>\n<li>The first <strong>56</strong> sessions are entirely repeat free, so the first repeat occurs in session <strong>57</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each table seats either <strong>6</strong>, <strong>5</strong>, or <strong>0</strong> people during each particular session.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Considering that in the real world, you will not have perfect attendance/adherence anyways, this is probably mostly indistinguishable from a truly perfect schedule for the participants.</p>\n<p>You can look at the schedule <a href=\"https://www.groupmixer.app/share#gm=br3.G9Y0AGYA9Ox5CSLmCTESo8SMVRRFx5fU2CVNghhNiJEYRe2OnxARKY5I0iRGiRiJcarl0PGNVTqs0fZQo6XjXa_d__5vamMC2t2ntXo-NhYABRqoBhZ4SgFlbo3pqSIE0nQXPdqeQtf9Qk4BAAfga4hUhCciOR1hh8hDeDrbIXIScaMze0dXe2cXrKO756kBsjl4eLgRDCMy0GzGVbGuHgsuMyFZd8cWJYfo6C_jRXazc421U4WdPCja0W5OXG8h9l2wHBFXEJ7R1xAhBxSF-G25kVKUeNmZ9eWzFhs7hH9vCE8nxxvMLAE7zb4iO9yDHiVJRKRgZMT5xDzKBvD0G5t0tew8TjvaIXIf0Z7ycHRTQVTmuiPFa1hlAgIlx6w6fYqlU2tnR5NPUPmW7dXERHJrnU47O82QT5w9HF1dnVzdb9yAzc6DQoQwbn8IJG6wFPzIkovjaVdHN1IS4sZbIuAMYXFggU4o9hX1bJk-T1UwBNNh5Y66yWEZMOsKnON76fmWXGEQy-WZPtI0QuvgqDK1xLSZoxkYPdTOCESBXP45dZ2uHzsmrw_7HXiiSC56VFneN13wt-eLOX0rc2Ld6UKEu5F1n5yIm4lvLz_ys05WYixmNzR6tIWzW20M8FFF3X_6D1-ZGlArhAqaPQGKmORwXNU