NeB53dQwM-sDqw0VPV8pxd0lNEMyzbdT2Ms-2xBM5wt-CtoS3Gi7SYpRtBuqmhv-5L2eZWGW__NjU7P7BPeIxpqLaJhE\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a>. <sup>(The schedule is too long to include directly in this answer. It would exceed the 30000 character answer limit.)</sup></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> I am the developer of the linked web-tool, and I used it to compute the schedule. The link is long because it actually contains a compressed version of the result, it is stored directly in the link and not dependent on a server.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For finding a schedule which fulfills your requirement exactly, you are at the mercy of the available design theoretical construction methods for your particular parameters. If you have wiggle room with those, you can choose some for which more favorable constructions exist and go with those.</p>\n<p>One example I can give is this: If you allow group sizes of <strong>8</strong> people, which is a reasonable assumption now that the pandemic is over and you say the tables have a capacity of <strong>12</strong>, you can get a result with these properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Over <strong>41</strong> sesions, everyone meetes exactly once.</li>\n<li>Every session, each of the <strong>36</strong> tables seat either <strong>8</strong>, <strong>7</strong>, or <strong>6</strong> people, the remaining ones stay empty.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can generate such a schedule instantly by clicking this <a href=\"http://www.groupmixer.app/share#gm=br3.G1cCQJwHdoOzsQr_RMA5mWjKo71oCGibLNXLbwYlL4lbETS1DktXX1uefneBQh8lBdRkNxloQDgDntsYnvC0y6c7etK3qsUWLPfEA05wAyH6qiNNlC60rW-8abxXJrkOMcOXwxXBCk5TuGIES3S7h_tuJ4pI_TAXR3S_mHuBB9nO-jMf2qaGqW7CSRfW_HU6JePSiBMjFsnNGpY8fXePrzBc_IGVSaedhnpaZhw\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">link</a>.</p>\n", "tags": ["arrays", "vba", "ms-access", "mathematical-optimization", "apl"]}
{"id": "3326abeb-60f4-4895-af15-b448f2fb8031", "text": "Q: How to obtain the absolute path of a file via Shell (BASH/ZSH/SH)? A: <p>In zsh, yes there is.</p>\n<p>Assuming your path is stored in a variable, you can use the <code>:P</code> modifier to get the absolute path of the file.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code>f='../example.txt'\necho ${f:P}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The above will print the absolute path of <code>../example.txt</code>.</p>\n<p>You can alternatively use the <code>:A</code> modifier to further resolve symbolic links, but this depends on <code>realpath</code> being installed.</p>\n<pre><code>f='../example.txt'\necho ${f:A}\n</code></pre>\n<p>See <a href=\"https://zsh.sourceforge.io/Doc/Release/Expansion.html#Modifiers\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://zsh.sourceforge.io/Doc/Release/Expansion.html#Modifiers</a></p>\n", "tags": ["bash", "shell", "path"]}
{"id": "04021b55-bcf2-455b-a6ee-1ae47f82c0fe", "text": "Q: How to solve the following issue in Spark 3.0? Can not create the managed table. The associated location already exists.; A: <p>It looks like you run your test data generation and your actual test in the same process - can you just replace these with <strong>createOrReplaceTempView</strong> to save them to Spark's in-memory catalog instead of into a Hive catalog?</p>\n<p>Something like : <strong>batchDF.createOrReplaceTempView(&quot;mytable&quot;)</strong></p>\n", "tags": ["apache-spark", "spark-streaming", "spark3"]}
{"id": "025b2c3a-a7f7-40a6-9c56-5fa512a15c3c", "text": "Q: Spark Job failing with exit status 15 A: <p>This Error mostly occurs while you submitting the wrong spark parameter in the spark-submit command. Please check the configuration parameters. In my case, I failed to submit masternamenode address where I need to read resources from HDFS.</p>\n<p>Before : Where yarn threw Error- 15\ndfs://masternamenode/TESTCGNATDATA/</p>\n<p>After: Able to run the application\nhdfs://masternamenode/TESTCGNATDATA/</p>\n", "tags": ["scala", "hadoop", "apache-spark"]}
